# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 has nothing from it; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 have "□occurs → ¬free"; they differ when only "there is freedom of the will" and "necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true, which the first allows
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 with no modality; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there is freedom of the will", "necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" and "necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true. Is S3, "Wherefore, if from eternity He foreknows not only what men will do, but also their designs and purposes, there can be no freedom of the will, seeing that nothing can be done, nor can any sort of purpose be entertained, save such as a Divine providence, incapable of being deceived, has perceived beforehand.", true then?
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 has "free → ◇¬occurs"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "there is freedom of the will" is true, which the second allows
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 put it at S3, "Wherefore, if from eternity He foreknows not only what men will do, but also their designs and purposes, there can be no freedom of the will, seeing that nothing can be done, nor can any sort of purpose be entertained, save such as a Divine providence, incapable of being deceived, has perceived beforehand."; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 at S1, "'It seems,' said I, 'too much of a paradox and a contradiction that God should know all things, and yet there should be free will."
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S3, "Wherefore, if from eternity He foreknows not only what men will do, but also their designs and purposes, there can be no freedom of the will, seeing that nothing can be done, nor can any sort of purpose be entertained, save such as a Divine providence, incapable of being deceived, has perceived beforehand.", rather than S1, "'It seems,' said I, 'too much of a paradox and a contradiction that God should know all things, and yet there should be free will."?
